-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  lex_splices.pcpp - Lexical compliance: newlines and line splices

  A newline is LF, CR LF or a lone CR (BNF.md sec 9). A backslash right
  before a newline is a line splice: it is removed before tokens are read,
  so it joins an identifier (LVa\ + alue = LValue), a string, and a //
  comment with the next line (that println is part of the comment). The
  last three printlns stand on lines that end in CR LF, CR and LF.

  How to check, from repo\bin:
    pcpp res\tests\lex_splices.pcpp -r
    pcpp res\tests\lex_splices.pcpp -r -t x86_64-linux-gnu   (runs in WSL)
  EXPECT:
    7
    abcd
    crlf
    cr
    lf
    [Heap] Allocs: 0, Frees: 0, Leaked: 0
  EXITCODE: 0
  The [Heap] line is the debug build's heap report.

  Break it: run each file below the same way, without -r; each reports
  exactly what the // comment on its marked line says, and nothing else:
    lex_splices_err_space.pcpp
      a space between the backslash and the newline (LX0201, a warning: still a splice)
    lex_splices_err_space_comment.pcpp
      the same in a // comment (LX0201, a warning, at the backslash)
    lex_splices_err_cppstart.pcpp
      a cppstart block whose last line ends in a splice (LX0014)
*******************************************************************************/

module exe lex_splices;

var
  LValue: int32 = 7;

begin
  println("{}", LVa\
lue);
  println("ab\
cd");
  // a comment that goes on \
  println("swallowed by the comment");
  println("crlf");
  println("cr");  println("lf");
end.
